ation of the two which  would be interesting but it costs a lot  to move from where we are now in terms  of using different type of fuel if you  like in those very heavy energy  intensive sectors like for example  steels and chemicals and cement where  the costs are  very very great in terms of moving into  something else and they use huge amount  of heat for the process of production  that they've got at present so it is  going to be difficult it will require  a lot more investment than what we've  got planned at present  um so what do you think given what we're  seeing at the moment um not having to  rely on gas imports it sounds like it  would be an excellent thing  uh yes it would be and uh i don't think  it's that much of a surprise that these  household names have called for it  because  the government's statutory body on uh  advising and among climate change the  committee on climate change actually  says that unabated gas should be over by  2035. so it's actually consistent with  what the messages that the government is  getting and what we are seeing  here is is a kind of a difference  between what the  most cost effective and the least  disruptive  way forward that the committee on  climate changes are laying out and the  policies that have been put in place and  not been put in place in a timely manner  to get us there and  i think on fossil fuels  there was a great report out last week  from oxford university that showed that  for fossil fuels  that are the costs that are adjusted for  inflation  they have not got any cheaper over the  last  140 years  whereas renewables have been decreasing  in price incredibly rapidly they are 2  000 times  cheaper today than they were  50 years ago so it makes sense to get  onto renewables  and off fossil fuels and vicky is  correct in that there are some  industrial processes that require uh  huge amounts of heat but carbon-free  production of steel has already started  in sweden and the uk needs to get at the  begin